#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133520 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9316ee08-13ad-57cf-87cf-2c7a86645aca" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Matrix that Defines an Orthogonal Projection Onto the Subspace Spanned by a Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "a348815b-cc51-569b-bcc7-d46ccc40fc68" :question/math-academy-id "e-5577" :question/problem "Find a matrix that defines the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto the subspace spanned by $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the matrix\n\n$$\nP_S = A(A^T\\!A)^{-1}\\!A^T\n$$\n\nmaps any vector $\\mathbf x\\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ onto its orthogonal projection in the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of the matrix $A$.\n\nIn our case, $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{S} &= A(A^{T}A)^{-1}A^{T} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix})^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot (14)^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -6 \\\\ -3 & -6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133521 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e81030a-0151-5dea-b9bc-74aae2499482" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Orthogonal Projection Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "5f72c4ca-3bd8-5398-8dc4-ee790127f844" :question/math-academy-id "e-5578" :question/problem "Which of the following are orthogonal projection matrices?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $Q$ is an orthogonal projection matrix if and only if $Q^2=Q$ and $Q^T=Q$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$ is *not* an orthogonal projection matrix. We have\n$A^T = A$\nbut\n$A^{2}|= A \\cdot A; = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 10\\end{bmatrix}; \\ne A$.\n- $B$ is *not* an orthogonal projection matrix. We have\n$B^{T} \\ne B$.\n- $C$ is an orthogonal projection matrix. Indeed,\n$C^T = C$\nand\n$C^{2}|= C \\cdot C; = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}; = \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}20 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 5\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}; = C$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133522 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0741fad6-3e88-53a4-bb5d-f80e445f8778" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Projection Matrix Given Vectors That Span the Orthogonal Complement"}] :question/id #uuid "ac4c3c85-2831-57b2-bef7-5be9c61ea365" :question/math-academy-id "e-5580" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nGiven that the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ above spans $S^\\perp$, and that $P$ is the orthogonal projection matrix of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto the subspace $S$, what is the value of $p_{13} + p_{32}$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the orthogonal projection matrix onto\n\n$$\nS^\\perp = \\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the orthogonal projection matrix\n\n$$\nP_{S^\\perp} = \\mathbf{a} (\\mathbf{a}^T\\!\\mathbf{a})^{-1} \\mathbf{a}^T\n$$\n\nwe obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{S^{⊥}} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 6^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\nP_S + P_{S^\\perp} = I\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{S} &= I - P_{S^{⊥}} \\\\\n&= I - \\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\np_{13} + p_{32} = (-2) + (-2) = -4\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133599 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9fd0121-5ce9-5a7b-a706-59ba73a71f7a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Matrix That Defines an Orthogonal Projection Onto the Subspace Spanned by a Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "57eab3ab-095d-5ba4-b071-7053e7a7df0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8316" :question/problem "Find a matrix that defines the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto the plane whose parametric equation is $\\mathbf{r}=s\\mathbf{v} + t\\mathbf{w}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the matrix\n\n$$\nP_S = A(A^T\\!A)^{-1}\\!A^T\n$$\n\nmaps any vector $\\mathbf x\\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ onto its orthogonal projection in the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of the matrix $A$.\n\nNotice that the plane\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=s\\mathbf{v}+t\\mathbf{w}\n$$\n\nis actually the subspace $\\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w}\\}$.\nHence, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{S} &= A(A^{T}A)^{-1}A^{T} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134178 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9316ee08-13ad-57cf-87cf-2c7a86645aca" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Matrix that Defines an Orthogonal Projection Onto the Subspace Spanned by a Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adf53571-51ca-513b-b71c-6aef969db260" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131511} {:answer/id #uuid "e3bab1a9-9d75-5cc1-8a11-b2dc9276ca9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131512} {:answer/id #uuid "13df6a52-952d-5737-8dac-19939159ccd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 & 9 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131513} {:answer/id #uuid "3e523716-bb03-57c3-ac95-268100eda397" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -6 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131514} {:answer/id #uuid "7ecea3d1-851b-50d5-ad73-2ad731aeaa24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 9 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e523716-bb03-57c3-ac95-268100eda397" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{14}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -6 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131514} :answer-field/id #uuid "eda662f4-6150-5f8c-86d7-4a6774ba9250" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131510}] :question/id #uuid "3e8bbee6-c0e5-5700-9398-9ec78200b13b" :question/math-academy-id "q-40128" :question/problem "Find a matrix that defines the orthogonal projection of $R^{3}$ onto the subspace spanned by $a = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134179 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0741fad6-3e88-53a4-bb5d-f80e445f8778" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Projection Matrix Given Vectors That Span the Orthogonal Complement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86fd0a39-c35d-559f-b661-ac8c84357134" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131517} {:answer/id #uuid "c0cfe70a-7e9e-5536-9db6-0affbb596dc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131518} {:answer/id #uuid "e8b20326-ac10-5286-8733-a5f2e113a56d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131519} {:answer/id #uuid "ec024c5d-6ac8-58a4-af37-ebd8d03ea3c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131520} {:answer/id #uuid "4a65cdb9-c555-59db-bd05-2832eef990ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86fd0a39-c35d-559f-b661-ac8c84357134" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131517} :answer-field/id #uuid "863e3f72-959e-57e3-9894-18eb4a82f02f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131516}] :question/id #uuid "2e5c1ea3-3ca4-5b38-a2fb-a8dacde3ab4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-40164" :question/problem "$a = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, P = \\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}p_{11} & p_{12} & p_{13} \\\\ p_{21} & p 22 & p_{23} \\\\ p 31 & p_{32} & p_{33}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nGiven that the vector $a$ above spans $S^{⊥}$, and that $P$ is the orthogonal projection matrix of $R^{3}$ onto the subspace $S$, what is the value of $p_{22} + p_{31}$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134228 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e81030a-0151-5dea-b9bc-74aae2499482" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Orthogonal Projection Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92da780e-d25b-5d50-948f-da4686c4167a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131811} {:answer/id #uuid "a2b6cb2a-d642-58eb-a261-cae8adaf7501" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131812} {:answer/id #uuid "c6c92d44-a544-54ca-b626-dcabe5759fd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131813} {:answer/id #uuid "d93230e5-949f-567d-96cd-f447439526dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131814} {:answer/id #uuid "41381956-ae74-5b0e-8bd7-17c38eb0c126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d93230e5-949f-567d-96cd-f447439526dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131814} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec2d2e10-42b4-507f-aa79-ed3ca53e8410" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131810}] :question/id #uuid "04ff9ec0-bc04-5bc6-a60f-5b7459d9c0ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-40662" :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A^{T} = A$\n2. $A^{2} = A$\n3. $A$ is an orthogonal projection matrix"}]}
